# The four bases, one on each compass point at radius 100, flat on the
# ground as `flagbuffet.bzw`'s are. Colour follows `hix.bzw`'s convention:
# north red, east green, south blue, west purple.
#
# Each base has a `team`-zoned pad just beyond it, tinted that team's colour
# the way a flag's pad is tinted the flag's -- so a first spawn lands on the
# base itself (`restartOnBase`, set on join and after a capture) and every
# other death lands on the pad instead, the same fallback rogue's centre zone
# answers. See docs/bzw.md, "Team zones". The same zone also carries `safety`
# for its team, so a flag dropped on an enemy base lands back on its own pad
# instead of the map centre.
base
  name n_red_base
  position 0 100 0
  size 20 20 0
  color 1
end

zone
  position 0 130 0
  size 20 8 0
  team 1
  safety 1
end
# A flush pad painting the zone above -- `color` on a box forces upstream's
# own general mesh path (CustomBox.cxx: any material property sets
# `isOldBox = false`, the same as a face selector or `phydrv`), and a truly
# flush (`size ... 0`) box in that path degenerates into four zero-area side
# faces, which real bzfs drops with an "invalid mesh face" warning per box --
# harmless (the pad is still flat and still visible from above; only its
# invisible vertical edge is at stake) but noisy. `0.01` keeps every one of
# these four pads visually flush while giving upstream a real plane to work
# with; `passable` is already stated outright, so nothing about drivethrough/
# shootthrough leans on bzo's own "h === 0" auto-detection either way.
box
  name n_red_spawn
  position 0 130 0
  size 20 8 0.01
  color 1.0 0.0 0.0
  passable
end

base
  name e_green_base
  position 100 0 0
  size 20 20 0
  color 2
end

zone
  position 130 0 0
  size 8 20 0
  team 2
  safety 2
end
box
  name e_green_spawn
  position 130 0 0
  size 8 20 0.01
  color 0.0 1.0 0.0
  passable
end

base
  name s_blue_base
  position 0 -100 0
  size 20 20 0
  color 3
end

zone
  position 0 -130 0
  size 20 8 0
  team 3
  safety 3
end
box
  name s_blue_spawn
  position 0 -130 0
  size 20 8 0.01
  color 0.101960784 0.2 1.0
  passable
end

base
  name w_purple_base
  position -100 0 0
  size 20 20 0
  color 4
end

zone
  position -130 0 0
  size 8 20 0
  team 4
  safety 4
end
box
  name w_purple_spawn
  position -130 0 0
  size 8 20 0.01
  color 1.0 0.0 1.0
  passable
end

# Four obstacle tests, one per corner, clear of the rings and the bases.

# North-east: the octagon and a thin wall, `hix.bzw`'s own third-person
# camera geometry, also carried in `flagbuffet.bzw`. Four planks at one
# position, 45 degrees apart; the half-width is the half-depth times
# (1 + sqrt 2), which is what puts each plank's corners exactly on its
# neighbour's. Near the middle a tank or a chase camera is inside as many as
# four obstacles at once.
box
  name oct_0
  position 105 105 0
  rotation 0
  size 14.485281374 6 12
end

box
  name oct_45
  position 105 105 0
  rotation 45
  size 14.485281374 6 12
end

box
  name oct_90
  position 105 105 0
  rotation 90
  size 14.485281374 6 12
end

box
  name oct_135
  position 105 105 0
  rotation 135
  size 14.485281374 6 12
end

# A wall a metre thick, thinner than the chase camera's trail, so backing
# into it puts the eye out beyond the far face instead of inside the wall.
#
# Also bzo's example of an external texture URL (docs/bzw.md, "Materials and
# appearance"): the one real map found to use one names this exact picture,
# on `images.bzflag.org`, upstream's own trusted default host -- but that host
# sends no `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` header today, so every client's own
# browser refuses the load and this wall falls back to bzo's plain `boxwall`.
# Left in on purpose rather than reverted, so the wall starts showing the
# real picture the moment (if ever) that changes, with nothing here to update.
box
  name thin_wall
  position 105 65 0
  rotation 0
  size 15 0.5 10
  texture http://images.bzflag.org/astevens/pine.png
end

# The same octagon again, but as one real mesh instead of four overlapping
# boxes -- same footprint (its 8 vertices are `oct_0`..`oct_135`'s own
# corners, local to this `define` so `group`'s own `position` below does the
# placing, `docs/bzw.md`'s "Groups" the same way `Watchtower` further down
# does) and the same 0..12 height, so driving into it and standing in it are
# a direct A/B against the plank emulation above. The eighth-dimension
# (`OO`) point is the real difference `docs/bzw-plan.md`'s "Mesh geometry"
# still calls out: a tank near the planks' shared centre can be inside as
# many as four separate obstacles at once (each with its own eighth-
# dimension node), where this is one obstacle and, once meshes get an
# eighth-dimension node of their own, would be one node.
define Octagon
mesh
  name Prism
  vertex -14.485281 -6.000000 0
  vertex -6.000000 -14.485281 0
  vertex 6.000000 -14.485281 0
  vertex 14.485281 -6.000000 0
  vertex 14.485281 6.000000 0
  vertex 6.000000 14.485281 0
  vertex -6.000000 14.485281 0
  vertex -14.485281 6.000000 0
  vertex -14.485281 -6.000000 12
  vertex -6.000000 -14.485281 12
  vertex 6.000000 -14.485281 12
  vertex 14.485281 -6.000000 12
  vertex 14.485281 6.000000 12
  vertex 6.000000 14.485281 12
  vertex -6.000000 14.485281 12
  vertex -14.485281 6.000000 12
  texture boxwall
  face
    vertices 0 1 9 8
  endface
  face
    vertices 1 2 10 9
  endface
  face
    vertices 2 3 11 10
  endface
  face
    vertices 3 4 12 11
  endface
  face
    vertices 4 5 13 12
  endface
  face
    vertices 5 6 14 13
  endface
  face
    vertices 6 7 15 14
  endface
  face
    vertices 7 0 8 15
  endface
  face
    vertices 7 6 5 4 3 2 1 0
    texture roof
  endface
  face
    vertices 8 9 10 11 12 13 14 15
    texture roof
  endface
end
enddef

group Octagon
  position 50 105 0
end

# North-west: two overlap tests. A pyramid, a box and a flipped pyramid
# stand edge to edge -- climb the first, cross the flat top of the second,
# and the third flares out above you rather than tapering to a point. Below
# them, three pyramids share one footprint at three different sizes, so a
# tank can only ever be resting on the outermost one still taller than it.
pyramid
  name overlap_up
  position -95 83 0
  rotation 0
  size 18.0 9.0 10.0
end

box
  name overlap_mid
  position -95 95 0
  rotation 0
  size 18.0 3.0 10.0
end

pyramid
  name overlap_down
  position -95 107 0
  rotation 0
  size 18.0 9.0 -10.0
end

pyramid
  name nest_base
  position -95 55 0
  rotation 0
  size 10.0 10.0 3.0
end

pyramid
  name nest_mid
  position -95 55 0
  rotation 0
  size 5.0 5.0 7.0
end

pyramid
  name nest_top
  position -95 55 0
  rotation 0
  size 2.0 2.0 12.0
end

